INT. MESS TENT, FORWARD CAMP - DAY

A canvas tent flapping in dry wind. Trestle tables, tin plates, a single coffee urn. The distant rumble of artillery has stopped. Everything has stopped.

SERGEANT MORRIS HALE, 50s, grizzled, sits alone at the end of a bench, writing a letter in pencil. A tin mug steams at his elbow.

He writes. He stops. He crosses a line out. He writes again.

The tent flap parts. PRIVATE ELLIOT QUAYLE, 19, enters, helmet off, cropped hair, a young face wrapped around old eyes. He carries a rifle by the sling and a folded paper in his teeth. He takes the paper out and stands at attention.

QUAYLE
Sergeant Hale.

HALE
At ease, Private.

QUAYLE
Sir, I've been told to give you this.

He holds out the paper. Hale does not take it. He sets down the pencil.

HALE
Sit.

QUAYLE
Sergeant, I'm supposed to deliver it and come straight back.

HALE
Then sit and come straight back after.

Quayle sits. Hale slides the mug across the table. Quayle takes it. They both watch the tent flap moving.

HALE (CONT'D)
Quiet out there.

QUAYLE
Yes, Sergeant.

HALE
How long?

QUAYLE
Since noon.

HALE
Is that long?

QUAYLE
Longest it's been since I got here.

HALE
How long have you been here?

QUAYLE
Six weeks.

HALE
That's not long.

QUAYLE
No, Sergeant.

Hale picks up the paper, unfolds it, reads, folds it again. He sets it on the table beside his letter.

HALE
Do you know what this says?

QUAYLE
No, Sergeant.

HALE
It says the war is over.

QUAYLE
Sir?

HALE
Armistice at noon. That's why it's quiet.

Quayle sits very still. He sets the mug down. His hands lie flat on the table.

QUAYLE
Sergeant, nobody told us.

HALE
They're telling us now.

QUAYLE
Is it true?

HALE
It's signed.

QUAYLE
Then why do we still have to stand watch?

HALE
Because nobody told the other side.

QUAYLE
(softly)
Oh.

HALE
Don't take your helmet off yet.

QUAYLE
No, Sergeant.

HALE
You can take your boots off.

QUAYLE
Sir?

HALE
I've never seen a man win a war with his boots on.

Quayle laughs, helpless, and puts his hands over his face. Hale lifts the pencil and finishes his sentence. The tent flap moves. The wind carries a distant bugle, very thin, very far.

QUAYLE
Sergeant. Is that ours?

HALE
It's somebody's.

QUAYLE
What do we do now?

HALE
We eat, Private.

QUAYLE
And after?

HALE
After, you write to your mother.

QUAYLE
What do I tell her?

HALE
(not looking up)
Tell her you were in the mess tent when it happened.
